## The terraform project

### Engine directory

The core terraform files are located in `./engine`, including `main.tf`, `variables.tf` and `outputs.tf`.
Standalone resources are defined in `./engine/modules` as standard terraform modules.

All variables MUST be defined in `./engine/variables.tf` without default values; defaults live in
`./engine/presets/defaults-all.tfvars`. All `local-exec` provisioners MUST set
`interpreter = ["/bin/bash", "-c"]`.

### Presets
`./engine/presets` correspond to optional sets of default variables. Users SHOULD NOT modify these files.

## Deploying an inference workload (consumer onboarding)

This cluster runs on an **endpoints-only VPC**: nodes reach only this account's regional
ECR and S3 — no public internet. So a workload's images and weights must be **rehosted
into our ECR/S3 before you deploy it**. The `onboarder` CodeBuild job does this. It
auto-detects two input formats and emits the matching air-gapped artifact:

| Path | Input (a tarball) | Deploy with | Emits | Drift reconciliation |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A — Helm chart** | `Chart.yaml` + `values.yaml` (`images:`/`weights:` blocks) | `helm install -f overrides.yaml` | `overrides.yaml` | none (Helm renders only) |
| **B — KRO graph** | `graph.yaml` (RGD) + `values.yaml` (field-path lists) | `kubectl apply` the graph + a CR | `graph-air-gapped.yaml` | **continuous** (KRO reconciles children) |

### Path A — Helm chart

**A1. Make your chart conform.** Every image is a structured object under `images:` and
every template renders it through the shared `chart.image` helper — never hardcode a
registry ref (a hardcoded ref has no pull path here and the onboard job rejects it):

```yaml
# values.yaml
images:                              # MANDATED — every image an addressable object
  server:  { registry: "", repository: vllm/vllm-openai, tag: v0.6.3 }
  sidecar: { registry: "", repository: my/sidecar,        tag: "1.2" }
weights:                             # OPTIONAL — declare to have onboard rehost into our S3
  model:   { source: "hf://google/gemma-2-9b", name: gemma-2-9b }   # or source: s3://…
```

```gotemplate
{{/* templates/_helpers.tpl — copy this helper verbatim */}}
{{- define "chart.image" -}}
{{- $sep := ":" -}}{{- if hasPrefix "@" .tag -}}{{- $sep = "" -}}{{- end -}}
{{- if .registry -}}{{ .registry }}/{{ .repository }}{{ $sep }}{{ .tag }}
{{- else -}}{{ .repository }}{{ $sep }}{{ .tag }}{{- end -}}
{{- end -}}
```

```gotemplate
# in a Deployment template — render via the helper, never a literal ref
image: {{ include "chart.image" .Values.images.server }}
```

**A2. Onboard the chart.** Package it, upload to the input prefix, and start the job:

```bash
helm package ./my-chart -d /tmp                 # -> /tmp/my-chart-<ver>.tgz
IN=$(jd show --output onboarder_input_s3_uri --text)
OUT=$(jd show --output onboarder_output_s3_uri --text)
PROJECT=$(jd show --output onboarder_codebuild_project --text)
aws s3 cp /tmp/my-chart-*.tgz "$IN/my-chart.tgz"
aws codebuild start-build --project-name "$PROJECT" \
  --environment-variables-override "name=CHART_REF,value=$IN/my-chart.tgz,type=PLAINTEXT"
# when it succeeds, fetch the emitted overrides (rehost/out/<chart-name>/):
aws s3 cp "$OUT/my-chart/overrides.yaml" ./overrides.yaml
```

The job digest-vendors each `images:` entry to `<ecr>/workload/<repo>@<digest>`,
(optionally) ingests `weights:` into `models/<name>`, and **backstops** by rendering the
chart with the overrides and asserting every image resolves to our ECR — a convention
violation fails the build, not a hung pod.

**A3. Install with the overrides:**

```bash
jd cluster login
helm install my-gemma ./my-chart -f overrides.yaml --set replicas=4
```

### Path B — KRO graph (recommended for reconciled workloads)

Ship a **`graph.yaml`** (a valid KRO `ResourceGraphDefinition` with the **literal
upstream refs baked in**, so it also applies as-is on a non-air-gapped cluster) plus a
sidecar **`values.yaml`** that lists the *field-paths* onboard must rehost. Onboard reads
the ref at each path, vendors it, and writes a **rewritten copy** (`graph-air-gapped.yaml`)
— `graph.yaml` itself is never mutated.

**B1. Author the graph + the field-path sidecar.** The sidecar is two lists of bare
dotted/`[N]`-indexed paths into `graph.yaml`; the value at each path must be a *literal*
ref (not a `${...}` CEL expression). An `images:` path's value is a full image ref; a
`weights:` path's value is a source URI (`s3://…`/`hf://…`).

```yaml
# values.yaml (onboard sidecar — every OTHER value in graph.yaml is ignored)
images:
  - spec.resources[0].template.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image
weights:
  - spec.resources[0].template.spec.template.spec.containers[0].env[0].value
```

```yaml
# graph.yaml (excerpt) — literal refs at the listed paths; works non-air-gapped too
image: vllm/vllm-openai:v0.6.6                       # <- rehosted to <ecr>/workload/…@<digest>
env:
  - name: WEIGHTS_SOURCE
    value: s3://jumpstart-cache-prod-us-west-2/…/qwen2.5-7b   # <- rehosted to models/qwen2.5-7b
```

**B2. Onboard the graph.** Tar the dir (single top-level folder) and start the job — same
project, auto-detected as Path B:

```bash
tar -czf /tmp/my-graph.tgz -C ./ my-graph          # my-graph/{graph.yaml,values.yaml}
IN=$(jd show --output onboarder_input_s3_uri --text)
OUT=$(jd show --output onboarder_output_s3_uri --text)
PROJECT=$(jd show --output onboarder_codebuild_project --text)
aws s3 cp /tmp/my-graph.tgz "$IN/my-graph.tgz"
aws codebuild start-build --project-name "$PROJECT" \
  --environment-variables-override "name=CHART_REF,value=$IN/my-graph.tgz,type=PLAINTEXT"
# emitted under rehost/out/<graph metadata.name>/ :
aws s3 cp "$OUT/<graph-name>/graph-air-gapped.yaml" ./graph-air-gapped.yaml
```

Onboard **backstops** each listed path field-by-field (every image-path now resolves to
our ECR `@sha256:`, every weight-path to our S3) — cluster-independent, so a bad path or
a non-literal ref fails the build.

**B3. Apply the graph, then create CRs.** No Helm:

```bash
jd cluster login
kubectl apply -f graph-air-gapped.yaml             # registers the RGD + its CRD
kubectl apply -f my-cr.yaml                          # a ServableInference (etc.) instance
```

KRO's controller now reconciles the CR's children continuously.

## Batch S3 access

Batch workloads use the S3 API. Do not mount the intake or output bucket as a
PersistentVolume. The platform creates these resources in the workload namespace:

- The `batch-inference` service account gets credentials through EKS Pod Identity.
- The `batch-storage` ConfigMap contains the AWS Region and both bucket names.
- The role can list and read intake objects.
- The role can list, read, and write output objects.
- The role cannot delete objects or change either bucket.

Deploy each batch workload in the configured workload namespace. The default namespace is
`inference`. A Helm Deployment must use this pod configuration:

```yaml
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: batch-inference
      containers:
        - name: worker
          envFrom:
            - configMapRef:
                name: batch-storage
```

A KRO graph must put the same `serviceAccountName` and `envFrom` fields in each batch
pod template. The worker reads `BATCH_INTAKE_BUCKET` and writes
`BATCH_OUTPUT_BUCKET`. The AWS SDK and AWS CLI use the Pod Identity credentials
automatically. A producer uses a separate IAM identity to upload intake objects.

## GPU placement — choosing an accelerator tier

Karpenter provisions GPU nodes on demand from two pools. A workload pod picks a tier by
its `nodeSelector` + tolerations; per-GPU requests (`limits: nvidia.com/gpu: 1`) let
several pods bin-pack onto one node.

| Tier | NodeSelector | Tolerations the pod needs | Instances |
|---|---|---|---|
| **g** (default) | `inference/accelerator: nvidia-g` | `nvidia.com/gpu=present:NoSchedule` | g5/g6/g6e/g7 (A10G / L4 / L40S) |
| **p** (high-end) | `inference/accelerator: nvidia-p` | `nvidia.com/gpu=present:NoSchedule` **and** `inference/gpu-tier=high:NoSchedule` | p4d/p5/p5en (A100 / H100 / H200) |

```yaml
# a g-tier pod (most inference)
nodeSelector: { inference/accelerator: nvidia-g }
tolerations:
  - { key: nvidia.com/gpu, operator: Exists, effect: NoSchedule }
```
```yaml
# a p-tier pod (large models / high throughput) — opts in on BOTH the label and the tier taint
nodeSelector: { inference/accelerator: nvidia-p }
tolerations:
  - { key: nvidia.com/gpu, operator: Exists, effect: NoSchedule }
  - { key: inference/gpu-tier, operator: Equal, value: high, effect: NoSchedule }
```

**Cost-safety:** the P pool carries an extra `inference/gpu-tier=high` taint, so a pod
lands on (and provisions) an expensive P node ONLY when it explicitly opts in on both
axes. A GPU pod that forgets the P selector falls to the cheaper g pool — a P node is
never spun up by accident. The P pool is gated by `enable_gpu_p_nodepool` (default on;
the pool definition is inert until a pod opts in).

**Seams (not default):**
- **ODCR** — P on-demand capacity is scarce (you may hit `InsufficientInstanceCapacity`).
  Set `gpu_p_capacity_reservation_id` to pin the P pool to a Capacity Reservation.
- **Shared GPU pool** — when P is reserved/always-on, backfilling its idle GPUs with
  g-tier work is worthwhile (drop the tier taint so g-pods may land on P). NOT the
  default: for elastic on-demand P, backfilling pins the node up and blocks
  consolidation, usually raising cost.

